## What this covers

This licence applies **only to the original material in this repository**: the
rules text, the design, the prose, the tables, the layout, and the three metal
charts drawn by this project.

It does **not** and cannot cover the Mistborn setting, its terminology, its
characters, the Dungeons & Dragons rules, or the illustrations. Those belong to
other people. See [NOTICE](NOTICE) for what is whose.

Nothing here grants you any right to material this project does not own.

## The licence

The original material described above is licensed under the

**Creative Commons Attribution-NonCommercial-ShareAlike 4.0 International**
**(CC BY-NC-SA 4.0)**

https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/

In short, you may share and adapt it, provided you:

- **give credit**, link to the licence, and say whether you changed anything;
- do not use it **commercially**;
- release anything you build on it under **the same licence**.

Non-commercial is not a preference. This work rests on a setting owned by
somebody else, so there are no commercial rights here to grant.

## Why not a software licence

An earlier instinct was to reach for MIT, on the grounds that this project does
not own the underlying material. That reasoning runs backwards. A licence is a
grant made over work you own; it is not a disclaimer for work you do not. MIT
on this document would assert a right to sublicense the Mistborn setting, which
is a larger claim than saying nothing at all. MIT is also written for source
code and warranties, neither of which describes a rulebook.

Licence what is yours. Disclaim what is not. Those are two separate jobs, and
[NOTICE](NOTICE) does the second one.
